1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,325
Kallang Airport, 'the finest
airport in the British Empire'

2
00:00:04,325 --> 00:00:08,317
On the north bank of the Kallang
Basin, across the water from

3
00:00:08,397 --> 00:00:12,256
the Singapore Sports Hub, stands
a low, curved building the

4
00:00:12,336 --> 00:00:16,675
colour of pale butter, with a
round tower rising from its centre.

5
00:00:16,675 --> 00:00:21,675
Its address today is 9 Stadium
Link, just off Nicoll Highway.

6
00:00:21,675 --> 00:00:25,028
Tens of thousands of people
pass it every week on

7
00:00:25,108 --> 00:00:29,022
the way to a football match
or a run along the promenade,

8
00:00:29,102 --> 00:00:31,975
and most of them have no idea what it is.

9
00:00:31,975 --> 00:00:36,565
For eighteen years it was the
passenger terminal of Kallang Airport,

10
00:00:36,645 --> 00:00:39,861
Singapore's first
purpose-built civil aerodrome,

11
00:00:39,941 --> 00:00:44,050
described in its day as the
finest in the British Empire and,

12
00:00:44,130 --> 00:00:47,975
by Amelia Earhart, as "an
aviation miracle of the East."

13
00:00:47,975 --> 00:00:51,600
The airport closed in 1955.

14
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:54,360
The terminal is still there, still empty,

15
00:00:54,440 --> 00:00:58,250
still waiting for someone to
decide what to do with it.

16
00:00:58,250 --> 00:01:01,541
Before Kallang, civil aircraft arriving in

17
00:01:01,621 --> 00:01:05,232
Singapore shared the
Royal Air Force fields at

18
00:01:05,312 --> 00:01:09,325
Seletar and Sembawang,
both laid out in the 1920s.

19
00:01:09,325 --> 00:01:12,783
Civil aviation was growing
fast, and the colony

20
00:01:12,863 --> 00:01:16,175
wanted an airport of
its own, close to town.

21
00:01:16,175 --> 00:01:20,761
On the 31st of August 1931 the
Governor of the Straits Settlements,

22
00:01:20,841 --> 00:01:24,104
Sir Cecil Clementi,
announced where it would go:

23
00:01:24,184 --> 00:01:29,327
"an aerodrome which is equally suitable
for land planes and for sea planes;

24
00:01:29,407 --> 00:01:33,725
and the best site, beyond all
question, is the Kallang Basin."

25
00:01:33,725 --> 00:01:37,029
The basin was a tidal
swamp where the Kallang,

26
00:01:37,109 --> 00:01:39,824
Geylang and Rochor rivers met the sea,

27
00:01:39,904 --> 00:01:43,950
fringed by Malay villages
built on stilts over the mud.

28
00:01:43,950 --> 00:01:48,193
Turning it into an airfield took
roughly 300 acres of reclamation,

29
00:01:48,273 --> 00:01:51,927
filled largely with earth cut
from the surrounding hills,

30
00:01:52,007 --> 00:01:56,840
and the resettlement of the families
who lived there — many of them moved a

31
00:01:56,920 --> 00:02:01,375
short way inland to a new village
that took the name Kampung Melayu.

32
00:02:01,375 --> 00:02:05,325
The work ran for most of the 1930s.

33
00:02:05,325 --> 00:02:09,052
Kallang Airport opened
on the 12th of June 1937,

34
00:02:09,132 --> 00:02:15,000
when Sir Shenton Thomas, by then
Governor, declared it open to the public.

35
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:19,875
What made Kallang unusual was
that it did two jobs at once.

36
00:02:19,875 --> 00:02:23,004
Land planes used a large
circular grass field,

37
00:02:23,084 --> 00:02:27,609
which let them take off and land
into the wind from any direction;

38
00:02:27,689 --> 00:02:31,028
flying boats came down on
a marked-out stretch of

39
00:02:31,108 --> 00:02:34,875
the basin and taxied to a
slipway beside the terminal.

40
00:02:34,875 --> 00:02:38,417
That mattered in the 1930s,
because the trunk route

41
00:02:38,497 --> 00:02:42,109
between Britain and Australia
— the Imperial Airways

42
00:02:42,189 --> 00:02:46,015
and QANTAS "Empire" service
— was flown by Short Empire

43
00:02:46,095 --> 00:02:49,575
flying boats that put down
on water at each stop.

44
00:02:49,575 --> 00:02:52,750
Kallang was one of their scheduled halts.

45
00:02:52,750 --> 00:02:57,580
Smaller operators used the land
field: Wearne's Air Service for

46
00:02:57,660 --> 00:03:02,725
the Malayan towns, and the Dutch
airline KNILM across to Batavia.

47
00:03:02,725 --> 00:03:06,681
The terminal itself was the
work of Frank Dorrington Ward,

48
00:03:06,761 --> 00:03:10,230
the chief architect of the
Public Works Department,

49
00:03:10,310 --> 00:03:13,650
who would go on to design
the Old Supreme Court.

50
00:03:13,650 --> 00:03:17,488
It was built in the streamline
Moderne style of the moment —

51
00:03:17,568 --> 00:03:21,667
a symmetrical, gently curved
frontage with a cylindrical control

52
00:03:21,747 --> 00:03:25,650
tower at the centre and a public
viewing gallery on the roof,

53
00:03:25,730 --> 00:03:29,975
from which Singaporeans could
watch the aircraft for a small fee.

54
00:03:29,975 --> 00:03:33,525
The building drew
visitors in its own right.

55
00:03:33,525 --> 00:03:36,889
When Amelia Earhart stopped
at Kallang during her

56
00:03:36,969 --> 00:03:41,107
round-the-world attempt in June
1937, a few weeks before she

57
00:03:41,187 --> 00:03:45,043
disappeared over the Pacific,
she called it "an aviation

58
00:03:45,123 --> 00:03:49,049
miracle of the East"; in its
early years it was routinely

59
00:03:49,129 --> 00:03:52,925
described as the finest
airport in the British Empire.

60
00:03:52,925 --> 00:03:57,425
The airport had a short life,
and much of it was spent at war.

61
00:03:57,425 --> 00:04:01,554
By late 1941 the grass field
was RAF Station Kallang,

62
00:04:01,634 --> 00:04:06,398
and when the Japanese advanced
down Malaya it became the only

63
00:04:06,478 --> 00:04:10,925
fighter airfield on Singapore
island still in operation.

64
00:04:10,925 --> 00:04:14,842
Its Brewster Buffalo squadrons
were outmatched from the start;

65
00:04:14,922 --> 00:04:17,807
the Hawker Hurricanes
that replaced them fared

66
00:04:17,887 --> 00:04:21,175
a little better but were
too few and came too late.

67
00:04:21,175 --> 00:04:26,660
On the 9th of February 1942, with the
field cratered and under artillery fire,

68
00:04:26,740 --> 00:04:30,299
the surviving Hurricanes
were withdrawn to Sumatra,

69
00:04:30,379 --> 00:04:34,375
and Singapore spent its last
week without fighter cover.

70
00:04:34,375 --> 00:04:39,153
During the occupation the Japanese
military replaced the grass

71
00:04:39,233 --> 00:04:43,541
landing ground with a concrete
runway and extended it to

72
00:04:43,621 --> 00:04:48,400
5,500 feet — a hard runway the
pre-war airport had never had.

73
00:04:48,400 --> 00:04:52,389
Civil flights resumed properly
in 1949, and for a few

74
00:04:52,469 --> 00:04:56,534
years Kallang was busy again:
a Straits Times headline

75
00:04:56,614 --> 00:05:00,603
in April 1950 called it the
second-busiest airport in

76
00:05:00,683 --> 00:05:04,825
the Far East, handling the
propeller airliners of BOAC,

77
00:05:04,905 --> 00:05:09,050
KLM, Qantas, Air France and
the young Malayan Airways.

78
00:05:09,050 --> 00:05:13,568
The runway was lengthened once
more to take the first BOAC Comet

79
00:05:13,648 --> 00:05:19,100
jets in 1951, but hemmed in by the sea
on one side and the city on the other,

80
00:05:19,180 --> 00:05:23,275
it was never going to be long
enough for what was coming.

81
00:05:23,275 --> 00:05:27,900
The airport also saw its share
of the era's larger events.

82
00:05:27,900 --> 00:05:32,191
On the 13th of March 1954 a
BOAC Lockheed Constellation

83
00:05:32,271 --> 00:05:35,926
flying the Sydney–London
route came in short of

84
00:05:36,006 --> 00:05:41,012
the runway at Kallang, struck the
seawall and burst into flames;

85
00:05:41,092 --> 00:05:44,350
33 of the 40 people on board were killed.

86
00:05:44,350 --> 00:05:48,357
The public inquiry put the
crash down to a fatigued crew —

87
00:05:48,437 --> 00:05:53,501
the captain had been on duty more than
21 hours — and found the airport's

88
00:05:53,581 --> 00:05:57,950
fire and rescue service partly
responsible for the death toll.

89
00:05:57,950 --> 00:06:02,052
Two years later, on the 18th of
March 1956, some 20,000 people

90
00:06:02,132 --> 00:06:06,031
gathered at the terminal for
a merdeka (independence) rally

91
00:06:06,111 --> 00:06:09,943
addressed by Chief Minister
David Marshall; it turned into

92
00:06:10,023 --> 00:06:13,990
a riot as the crowd attacked
police and tried to force their

93
00:06:14,070 --> 00:06:18,050
way into the building, and at
least fifty people were hurt.

94
00:06:18,050 --> 00:06:20,890
There was no room to make Kallang bigger,

95
00:06:20,970 --> 00:06:24,175
so the government built
a replacement inland.

96
00:06:24,175 --> 00:06:28,812
Singapore International Airport
at Paya Lebar opened in 1955

97
00:06:28,892 --> 00:06:33,528
with a runway more than 8,000
feet long, and Kallang Airport

98
00:06:33,608 --> 00:06:38,325
closed to traffic that year,
eighteen years after it opened.

99
00:06:38,325 --> 00:06:42,151
The name lingered on the
map: the road along the old

100
00:06:42,231 --> 00:06:45,982
runway became Old Airport
Road, and the MRT station

101
00:06:46,062 --> 00:06:49,213
built there decades
later was named Dakota,

102
00:06:49,293 --> 00:06:53,950
after the Douglas DC-3 airliners
that had once used the field.

103
00:06:53,950 --> 00:06:58,100
The airfield never went back
to being anything small.

104
00:06:58,100 --> 00:07:00,997
Nicoll Highway was cut across the

105
00:07:01,077 --> 00:07:04,775
northern edge of the old airport in 1956.

106
00:07:04,775 --> 00:07:08,623
The terminal building and
its side blocks passed to the

107
00:07:08,703 --> 00:07:12,694
Singapore Youth Sports Council,
and from 1960 to 2009 the

108
00:07:12,774 --> 00:07:17,407
main building housed the headquarters
of the People's Association;

109
00:07:17,487 --> 00:07:20,978
between 1967 and 1972 part
of the premises was the

110
00:07:21,058 --> 00:07:24,835
Central Manpower Base, where
a generation of young men

111
00:07:24,915 --> 00:07:29,200
reported for their national
service medicals and enlistment.

112
00:07:29,200 --> 00:07:34,124
In 1973 the National Stadium opened
on what had been the runway area,

113
00:07:34,204 --> 00:07:38,258
and for the next three decades
the "Kallang Roar" and the

114
00:07:38,338 --> 00:07:43,125
"Kallang Wave" belonged to a
football ground built on an airfield.

115
00:07:43,125 --> 00:07:47,318
That stadium closed in
2007 and was pulled down;

116
00:07:47,398 --> 00:07:52,650
the Singapore Sports Hub opened
on the same ground in 2014.

117
00:07:52,650 --> 00:07:57,421
The terminal itself was restored
and reopened in March 1994 after

118
00:07:57,501 --> 00:08:01,749
conservation works costing
4.16 million Singapore dollars,

119
00:08:01,829 --> 00:08:07,570
with the green-tinted windows, the steps
down to the old Kallang Road entrance

120
00:08:07,650 --> 00:08:12,725
and the airport's "lion against a
coconut tree" emblem all put back.

121
00:08:12,725 --> 00:08:17,966
It was formally gazetted for
conservation on the 5th of December

122
00:08:18,046 --> 00:08:23,200
2008 and served as a venue for
the Singapore Biennale in 2011.

123
00:08:23,200 --> 00:08:26,959
Since the People's Association
moved out in 2009, though,

124
00:08:27,039 --> 00:08:31,404
it has mostly stood empty, and the
Urban Redevelopment Authority's

125
00:08:31,484 --> 00:08:35,849
long-term plans for the district
fold the building into a heritage

126
00:08:35,929 --> 00:08:40,160
and lifestyle precinct, provisionally
called Old Airport Square,

127
00:08:40,240 --> 00:08:44,550
within the wider redevelopment
of the area as Kallang Riverside.

128
00:08:44,550 --> 00:08:48,750
For now it is a listed
building in search of a tenant.

129
00:08:48,750 --> 00:08:52,470
Where it fits in the bigger
story: Singapore has kept the

130
00:08:52,550 --> 00:08:56,750
shell of Kallang Airport and lost
almost everything it was for.

131
00:08:56,750 --> 00:09:00,237
The terminal that once stood
for the colony's arrival in

132
00:09:00,317 --> 00:09:03,231
the modern world — the
rooftop viewing gallery,

133
00:09:03,311 --> 00:09:07,754
the Empire flying boats on the basin,
Earhart's "miracle of the East" —

134
00:09:07,834 --> 00:09:11,003
is now a conserved façade
waiting on a master plan,

135
00:09:11,083 --> 00:09:14,650
on ground far better known
for football than for flight.
